#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167805} :activity/id #uuid "dc2433b7-ae23-5d90-bbbf-4a05485b89d1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050447 :topic/id #uuid "49c998a8-44ce-526f-ad90-85d8ceda122b" :topic/math-academy-id 3171} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168154 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the *maximum* value of the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2 + 4x_2^2,\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraint $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1.$ This type of problem is known as a **constrained optimization problem**.   \n\nSince our quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ has no mixed term $x_1x_2$, we can find its maximum value for $\\| \\mathbf{x} || = 1$ using algebraic manipulation, as follows.\n\nFirst, notice that $x_1^2 \\geq 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n2x_1^2 \\leq 4x_1^2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$ is equivalent to $x_1^2 + x_2^2 =1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=4(1)=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no greater than $4.$ But before we conclude that $4$ is the maximum, we need to make sure that $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can actually attain the value $4.$ Can we find a vector $\\mathbf x,$ with  $\\| \\mathbf{x} || = 1,$ such that $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = 4?$\n\nIndeed, we can. Setting ![](images/af/afe399f75b9a73a1834b4d359e29ad4814bd99f81b1cf20ccaa211078dd19645.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}+4(1)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1,$  the maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ equals $4.$\n\nProceeding similarly, we can show that the minimum of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $2.$ \n\nGraphically, the situation can be visualized if we intersect the surface $z= {\\color{black}2x_1^2+4x_2^2}$ and the cylinder $\\color{black}x_1^2+x_2^2=1,$ as shown below:  \n\n![](images/39/39bfc05c9dd0733c1f4f867d21d1a5f018aacbf0ed91036a6f0981d701ba69d8.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf2f75a6-c5dc-5dc6-bcc2-1c89fec829de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7952 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b1874b80-07fb-5d4d-9fd9-46c46098d2a0" :step/math-academy-id 45741 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167806}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166890 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166491 :question/id #uuid "b8306278-726a-5c09-86cc-14631a6abb66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12659"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049450}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378759}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "396a0949-2648-520d-b31c-049c70bbd0c2" :step/math-academy-id 45742 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167807}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166891 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166492 :question/id #uuid "5b4117f3-62fb-55c6-8e23-0cadcd4a2ec5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12660"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503749}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "2dac84d2-443e-546b-baa3-7a9ee2e2ef2c" :step/math-academy-id 45745 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167808}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168155 :tutorial/content "We can find a quadratic form's maximum and minimum values even when it contains cross-product terms. For this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)\\mathbin{:}$*\n\n- *The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* \n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n*The maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$ *\n\n- *The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* \n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n*The minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$ *\n\nSuppose we are given the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=4x_1^2-2x_1x_2+4x_2^2.\n$$\n\nHow can we find a unit vector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}$ at which the maximum of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is attained?\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/0a/0af81fbf910d2f2bbe2ad26f297f57ce230548e13ad27c2974109066b9faaee5.svg)\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}} = 5.\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}},$ we need a unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=5.$ We start by computing $(A-5I)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/a2/a257df47b352c5534608079419a337ae51103dd7c7763fb73f4fd692f36fd47e.svg)\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n![](images/cb/cb08ba808e7b6d8a8412df8cafaa85e795f523b63450d8f4e1c3081740a46866.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "10db6c75-237c-5a28-b5a8-ac25c57cc82f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7953 :tutorial/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms"} :step/id #uuid "3892412b-7fe5-5337-9881-d9cb02031080" :step/math-academy-id 45748 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167809}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167809 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166892 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166493 :question/id #uuid "3cec995b-8918-5c48-927e-b0807a947518" :question/math-academy-id "e-12661"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050376}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503712}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "3eb3250a-641e-50c7-85ee-a4a11b6073b7" :step/math-academy-id 45749}] :activity/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166976}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503814} :activity/id #uuid "e9940d97-1df3-50ca-9b63-8f71c44b3c30" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050447 :topic/id #uuid "49c998a8-44ce-526f-ad90-85d8ceda122b" :topic/math-academy-id 3171} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48ff6dc6-4553-5e13-910b-8df5f5a3bb78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503117} {:answer/id #uuid "4b434ef3-0980-5783-ad7e-eefe4f77ca09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503121} {:answer/id #uuid "55b888f5-b538-5f15-a7af-99357234caef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503146} {:answer/id #uuid "e428a7ec-4298-5c6e-9db5-e04a6ff4716f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503447} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d57557-c662-5e95-a738-e083bf15044c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503452}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503121} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb2b65ea-ec2e-55f1-a3f9-78f34b463133" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df4de458-1c06-5aa0-8cdd-76f4342f8dfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-158531" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+7{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n7{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 4{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}+7{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge 4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=4(1)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge 4.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $4.$ But before we conclude that $4$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=4.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}+7{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4(1)+7(0)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $4.$"} :step/id #uuid "1b2ac5b1-579a-53c7-a81c-873a2e26baee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018c99a3-0395-5f65-a414-0b527b9b1fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275490} {:answer/id #uuid "2418fd71-994a-57d9-8058-e7d13fe938ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275795} {:answer/id #uuid "724c24fe-2a6a-593e-84fa-821ecbbea055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276512} {:answer/id #uuid "adf63806-8714-5759-974a-7ebb5953e7da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277040} {:answer/id #uuid "c1115abc-a234-5591-8bd4-08bbcb550a6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277193}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275795} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5356da2-3693-5794-9588-7fc99e91ebda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7a98e5a2-f222-532b-be08-4de02d1187ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-158533" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n6{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 3{x}_{2}^{2},\\,4{x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 3{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge 3{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=3(1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge 3.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $3.$ But before we conclude that $3$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=3.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3(1)^{2}+6(0)^{2}+4(0)^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "50d0a5e2-27c5-5e78-8d99-9791c1bba80d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fddd54e-2f69-5317-9273-59acf0420171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266424} {:answer/id #uuid "488b4f03-ec81-5e5e-88ac-1e1b03de77fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266792} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2ad35c-a214-5c83-bfd9-e7a6a53c9023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267099} {:answer/id #uuid "c18a481a-15ba-5133-9b96-62027df826c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267872} {:answer/id #uuid "ff581e6d-531a-50a8-93f1-45b62be1bbc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268394}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267872} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2163251-a2b2-5338-af76-79765630ea17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2bb923d-a428-53a1-96e5-4cae59c3d2d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-138064" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -2{x}_{1}^{2},\\,-{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge -2{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -2{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-2(1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -2.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-2.$ But before we conclude that $-2$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4(0)^{2}-(0)^{2}-2(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "b1725c7e-8f6e-55e7-9a16-5bee31832ebc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503819}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43ad65d6-22ea-5a70-a6fc-c7a394b78979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503111} {:answer/id #uuid "84bc382e-bb1a-53e0-9616-39f48f878732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503253} {:answer/id #uuid "85e05f05-a7d1-5ce5-afb1-7cfe2bb20cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503254} {:answer/id #uuid "957979fc-282e-5501-883c-530bce9d9ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503275} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8a228b-4bc9-587e-9614-77df0af9703d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503425}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503425} :answer-field/id #uuid "53d0c528-6bbe-5382-9d7c-bfcd745b246c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8706edb2-e78b-5b3f-a44f-7250c01b8006" :question/math-academy-id "q-138072" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bcdf84a6-243b-59c3-8ec6-fb98d7537f1b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503834}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53998127-850c-52ba-bb5b-642fd8be09d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503142} {:answer/id #uuid "646349e3-b930-50b5-8fa8-ca955067823e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503176} {:answer/id #uuid "79f97822-f108-5494-a037-0fa3ba02771b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503230} {:answer/id #uuid "9947b2d7-78d2-510d-a31f-a38e1ffbf62d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503286} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c637b0-037f-510d-bebd-8c09cb3851b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503286} :answer-field/id #uuid "08b28165-ff27-54aa-8d84-67be182b4e5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5347ea8e-b936-5670-b92f-f003075bcc63" :question/math-academy-id "q-138073" :question/problem "Find the  maximum value of $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "edd7862f-dc8a-5754-a8ab-99b0aeb8d1d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503770}}] :activity/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502978}]}
